  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)+4$ by $4$ units *down.* \n\nHence, if the point $(-2,3)$ lies on $y=f(x)+4$, then the point \n\n$$\n(-2,3-4)=(-2,-1)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/5f74a8d57dc1766912c4490ab156d0f7f2ed0bbc9b4dbb9e53ae46c48ceb8bb5.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(-2,3)$ lies on $y=f(x)+4$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2)+4 & =3 \\\\ f(-2) & =3-4 \\\\ f(-2) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=-2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(-2,-1)$ must lie on $y=f(x).$",
